tpm-keyring-unlock (1.7.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release 1.7.1. See JOURNAL.md for the full history.
  * The power-button LED service of 1.7.0 never started. systemd refused
    its throwaway user, whose name was the service's group's (status
    217/USER). tpm-keyring-unlock-configure noticed, took it out again
    and changed nothing.
  * It now runs as a system user of its own, tpm-keyring-led, made by its
    sysusers file: no login shell, no home, no password. A throwaway user
    (DynamicUser=) cannot reach the system bus where that is dbus-daemon,
    as on Debian and Ubuntu, so even with its name fixed it would not
    have worked there. To get the service, run
    tpm-keyring-unlock-configure --upgrade once and answer the LED
    question.

 -- Dmitrii <dmitrii.timoshenko16@gmail.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:42:05 +0100

tpm-keyring-unlock (1.7.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release 1.7.0. See JOURNAL.md for the full history.
  * On a Framework laptop, tpm-keyring-unlock-configure offers to blink
    the power button's LED while the fingerprint reader in it waits for a
    finger. It is a small sandboxed service with no root and no network.
    It follows fprintd's own finger-needed property and hands the LED back
    to the embedded controller afterwards. Asked only where that LED is
    present and a finger is enrolled. Idea and design from
    framework-fingerprint-led-blink by alejandroqh (Apache-2.0),
    reimplemented in bash. tpm-keyring-unlock-deconfigure takes it out.
  * tpm-keyring-unlock-configure --upgrade re-checks the configuration
    after a package upgrade and offers what the new version adds. It keeps
    the sealed secret, with nothing to type, while it still unseals through
    the new helper, and re-seals only if it does not. To be offered the LED
    after upgrading, run it once.

 -- Dmitrii <dmitrii.timoshenko16@gmail.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:25:47 +0100

tpm-keyring-unlock (1.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release 1.6.0. See JOURNAL.md for the full history.
  * tpm-keyring-unlock-configure can give polkit prompts (App Center and
    snap installs, other admin actions) a fingerprint line of their own,
    which the lock screen never reads. It is asked the other way round:
    answer n at "Keep polkit prompts password-only?" to add the line,
    Enter keeps polkit password-only. With it, an enrolled finger alone
    approves root-level changes. tpm-keyring-unlock-deconfigure takes it
    back out.
  * Asked only where polkit prompts have no fingerprint and a finger is
    enrolled. To be asked after upgrading, run
    tpm-keyring-unlock-configure once more. Answer n at "Overwrite?" to
    keep the sealed secret.

 -- Dmitrii <dmitrii.timoshenko16@gmail.com>  Fri, 23 Oct 2026 10:56:16 +0200

tpm-keyring-unlock (1.5.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Release 1.5.1. See JOURNAL.md for the full history.
  * Fedora's gdm-fingerprint, which ships with no keyring line, is wired
    now: the configure step adds the keyring lines with the module between
    them. None of the added lines can vote, so the stack lets in exactly
    whom it let in before.
  * Under SELinux the configure step loads a one-rule policy module so
    that GDM's logins can reach the TPM. Removing this package unloads it.
  * After upgrading, run tpm-keyring-unlock-configure once more. It
    re-checks the PAM stacks an older version wired. Answer n at
    "Overwrite?" to keep the sealed secret.

 -- Dmitrii <dmitrii.timoshenko16@gmail.com>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 21:10:08 +0200

tpm-keyring-unlock (1.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

